  "blockers": [
    "prepareCouplingApproach refuses a follower whose virtual link has a nonzero turn. On the mesh, pairs link in junction queues, and extendLink keeps that turn along the carriageway, so every pair at rest at E had a turned link.",
    "collectMember claims the assembly lane from its first block, and prepareExit claims the split lane through its last block and the next stopping block. A third pod that queues behind the pair takes the first cell of the assembly lane after the rear moves to E. This stopped 38 of 41 approaches on the loop.",
    "bindExits extends the exit closure over every resource that both members use, so it follows the shared route after the split until the routes diverge. On the loop the members share the trunk after the split, and the closure met foreign pods downstream. This stopped the other 3 approaches, each after the reservation plan passed.",
    "discoverCouplingApproaches keeps one attempt for each front until its next berth departure, so a refused pair does not try again on a later corridor."
  ],
  "candidateGap": "couplingApproachOwnedFrontier checks each resource of the current block of the front, including a From-node resource that the front released. A front at rest at the end of cell 0 of the assembly lane (E = 30 m on the 90 m variant) is refused with 'approach front lost its actual granted owner'. This is a refusal before any approach exists, not a fault. Later classified as a defect: the check also covered the junction of the cell, which releases at the block end. The change 'sim: Skip passed resources in the approach frontier' removes it. With the fix, the same variant gives no such refusal and 5 approaches, which adoption refuses on the exit closure (3) and the stopping room (2)."
}
